5443] [17592186557569 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186557569 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y+2z &=-4 \\\\ -2x+5y-14z &=22 \\\\ 2x-4y+10z &=-16.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186557569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f6291f54-74fa-5018-8da1-0d69f2fed16a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186557569 #edb/ref 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -14 & 22 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 10 & -16\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -14 & 22 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 10 & -16\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -14 & 22 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 10 & -16\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -12 & 18 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 10 & -16\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 10 & -16\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 8 & -12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3z &=4 \\\\ y-4z &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z$. That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z$.\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3z+4 \\\\ y &=4z+6 \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3z+4 \\\\ 4z+6 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186557573 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186115241.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186557573 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "d3ac02e4-4a1b-5e90-b15c-bf75d125145d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186569456 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-2y-1 \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569456 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569456 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06bab61c-40c1-5534-b5a3-b01a2dbdfa45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569501 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569501 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569501 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a116b39-7748-5b97-8286-409d809e42cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569518 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569518 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569518 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "33822258-829b-5084-adb7-be68d8b35521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569546 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569546 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569546 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4ae92370-bb9c-5f64-acc7-34346d0fb34d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569554 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186569554 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 175921